  <p><strong>What should a buyer specify before approving all-over printed leggings?</strong></p>

  <h2>Mark the difference between a repeat and a placement graphic</h2>
  <p>For a repeating print, supply the actual size of one repeat and mark motif direction. For a placement graphic, show where it should land on each panel and which edge you are measuring from. A large motif running through a side seam needs a specific matching instruction. A scatter motif can tolerate a different join if the buyer approves the appearance.</p>

  <h2>Approve the points people actually see</h2>
  <p>Mark each critical seam rather than asking for every panel to align. Check a sewn sample at the hip, side leg and waistband in a standing pose and under normal stretch. Look for a split motif, the wrong artwork orientation or a light line exposed at the seam. Photograph the approved sample with the seams visible.</p>

  <h2>Ask how artwork changes with size</h2>
  <p>Decide whether the artwork stays fixed in physical size or is resized or repositioned by garment size. Test the proposed approach on the ends of the planned range, especially if a motif must stay clear of a pocket or gusset. Resolve placement before costing a layout that may require more material or tighter cutting control.</p>

  <p>Industry reference: <a href="https://www.yogasew.com/articles/printed-leggings-sampling-guide">YogaSew’s printed leggings sampling guide</a>.</p>
